<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-sampling-distributions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Sampling Distributions</title>
    <p>
      We now have the setup of hypothesis testing. We make the
      hypotheses: null and alternative. We assume the null hypothesis.
      Then, so far by simulation, we generate random samples to
      compare with our own sample statistics. This lets us assign a
      <m>p</m>-value, which is a measure of how unlikely it would be
      generate the sample statistic under the null hypothesis.
      Finally, we compare the <m>p</m>-value against a threshold of
      significant to determine whether or not we rejec the null
      hypothesis.
  </p>
  <p>
    In this section, I want to focus in on one important part of this
    process: the random sample generate to compare to the sample
    statistic. In the previous section, we used simulation, but
    eventually we do want to rely on the statistical theory. In order
    to do that, we need some development of this important step, so
    that we no longer have to run simulations. This leads to the
    important definition of this section.
  </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          As always, we are in a situation where we are researching a
          particular population parameter (difference of means or
          proportions, single mean or proportion, some other similar
          calculation). We have a sample statistics which is the same
          calculation as the population paramere but drawn just from
          your sample statistics. We have hypotheses: null and
          alternative.
        </p>
        <p>
          We assume the null hypothesis to be true. Under that
          assumption, we can generate random samples: fake data sets
          created by random choices under the assumption of the null
          hypothesis. From each of these samples, we can calculate the
          sample statistic, so we get a bunch of fake, randomly
          generated sample statistics.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is a new dataset with only one numeric variable:
          the list of values of all these randomly generated sample
          statistics. This new dataset is called the sampling
          distribution.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
  <p>
    I believe the sampling distribution is possibly the trickiest
    single concept in this course. It’s layered deep below other
    concepts, all of which have to be clear to understand the sampling
    distribution. It also suffers from a difficult name: the word
    ‘sample’ is pretty heavily overused word in introductory
    statistics, so its quite unclear what ‘sampling’ really refers to
    in this definition. I want to spend this section trying to really
    clarify this important definition.
  </p>
  <p>
    To that end, let me go over the process once more in general, and
    the apply this to an example.
    <ul>
      <li>
        We have a research questions. Mathematically, the research
        question is some equation or inequality concerning the
        population parameter.
      </li>
      <li>
        We write down the null hypothesis and the alternative
        hypothesis.
      </li>
      <li>
        We gather data for the variable involved in the parameter.
        That data is our sample, or simply ‘the sample’.
      </li>
      <li>
        From that data, we do the calculation from the research
        question. This produces our sample statistics, or simply ‘the
        sample statistics’.
      </li>
      <li>
        We assume the null hypothesis.
      </li>
      <li>
        Under that assumption, we randomly generate samples. These do
        not replace our sample: they are random samples to be used in
        comparison.
      </li>
      <li>
        From the randomly generate samples, we calclate the sample
        statistic for each. These do not replace our sample
        statistics: again, they are random sample statistics to be
        used in comparsion.
      </li>
      <li>
        We gather all these sample statistics into a collection called
        the sampling distribution.
      </li>
      <li>
        We use the sampling distribution to determine the probability
        of producing our sample under the null hypothesis: the
        <m>p</m>-value.
      </li>
      <li>
      </li>
      <li>
        We compare that probability with a threshold of signifigance.
      </li>
      <li>
        If our sample statistics is sufficiently unlikely, we reject
        the null hypothesis and say that our sample statistic is
        significant and can be use to support the alternative
        hypothesis.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="section-probability-theory"/>, I have already
    calculated a sample distribution. In the main exmaple in that
    section, we were looking at promotion decision. The population
    paramers was the difference in promotion rates between men and
    women under a male manager. The null hypothesis said that there
    was no difference. I ran ten simulations, each generating
  </p>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="section-randomness"/> I said that continuous
    probability is normally used to model discrete probability in
    statistics. The same is true for the sampling distribution.
    Instead of doing simulations, I want to rely on the mathematical
    theory of probability, specifically continuous probability.
  </p>
</section>
